<!--
  No `package` attribute: Android Gradle Plugin 8 removed it outright and fails
  the build if it is present. The package comes from `namespace` in
  build.gradle.kts instead.

  This library declares no permission and no component. The only thing it
  contributes is the pair of backup rules below, and it contributes them rather
  than documenting them because a host that forgets this one produces the worst
  failure mode this package has.

  ## Why the library, and not the app

  The master key lives in the AndroidKeyStore and never leaves the device. Back
  up `shared_prefs/minilynx_secure_storage.xml` and restore it — onto a new
  phone, or onto the same one after a wipe — and the file arrives without the
  key that decrypts it. What the app then has is not an absent credential, which
  it knows how to handle, but ciphertext it cannot read: the store throws on
  open, the library discards and recreates it, and a user who restored a backup
  is quietly signed out. Worse, the same bytes are now sitting in a cloud
  backup, which is exactly where a credential should not be.

  Auto-backup is on by default for apps targeting API 23 and up, so this is the
  default behaviour of every host that does nothing.

  ## What a host has to do about it

  Usually nothing. If the host app declares its own `android:fullBackupContent`
  or `android:dataExtractionRules`, the manifest merger will refuse to pick a
  winner and fail the build with a message naming both — which is the intended
  outcome, because it is a decision the app owns and a build error is the only
  way to make it a decision at all. The fix is one line in the host's manifest:

      <application
        android:fullBackupContent="@xml/app_backup_rules"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/app_data_extraction_rules"
        tools:replace="android:fullBackupContent,android:dataExtractionRules">

  plus the same two `<exclude domain="sharedpref" path="minilynx_secure_storage.xml"/>`
  entries copied into the app's own rule files. `README.md` carries this with the
  full XML.

  A host that sets `android:allowBackup="false"` needs neither.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

  <application
    android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/minilynx_secure_storage_data_extraction_rules"
    android:fullBackupContent="@xml/minilynx_secure_storage_backup_rules" />

</manifest>
